    "faithful": true,
    "note": "Independence over the trusted equational calculus Ampas.EL.Derives. The second conjunct rules out independence by TRIVIALIZATION -- not by inconsistency: in pure equational logic a one-element model always exists, so a theory cannot be inconsistent in the model-theoretic sense, and the degenerate case to exclude is that every equation becomes derivable. If x = y were derivable, substitution would make any two terms equal. The earlier wording said \"inconsistent\" and was corrected on the reviewer's finding (2026-10-13). Reviewer also observed that the calculus has no separate constructor for substituting into a DERIVED equation, but that the rule is admissible by induction on derivations (compose substitutions at ax; refl/symm/trans/congr survive substitution), so no inferential power is missing.",
